<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- net11.0 is the repo default now (test project, samples, demos, anything
         that doesn't say otherwise). The old Harmony blocker is gone: I'm
         building SageFs.Harmony 2.4.2-sagefs.1 (Directory.Packages.props) from
         my LibHarmony fork's patched MonoMod (ci-pipeline.fsx's "restore
         harmony fork" stage), and its detours actually work on CoreCLR 11 —
         proved it by running WebAppHotReloadVerificationTests on net11 with
         zero skips before touching this file.

         The tool I actually ship (SageFs, SageFs.Core, SageFs.Host) doesn't
         just move to net11 though — it multi-targets $(SageFsTargetFrameworks).
         A dotnet tool nupkg built that way carries one tools/<tfm>/any payload
         per TFM, and `dotnet tool install` grabs whichever one matches your
         SDK. So a .NET 10 user still gets a net10.0 build, a .NET 11 user gets
         net11.0, and issue #131 (a net11-only package that .NET 10 users
         couldn't install) can't happen again. If a project needs to
         multi-target like this, clear the default with
         `<TargetFramework></TargetFramework>` and set
         `<TargetFrameworks>$(SageFsTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>`. -->
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <SageFsTargetFrameworks>net10.0;net11.0</SageFsTargetFrameworks>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- F# nullness analysis: surfaces nullability warnings at compile time
         (requires the newer FCS/SDK — the whole point of keeping those current).
         NoWarn NU1608/NU1902 for the pre-1.0/known-audited transitive pins. -->
    <CheckNulls>true</CheckNulls>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1608;NU1902</NoWarn>
    <Version>0.6.860</Version>
    <!-- Reproducible/tamper-evident restore: commit packages.lock.json per
         project and fail restore if it drifts from Directory.Packages.props
         without a regenerate. RestoreLockedMode is scoped to CI so local
         `dotnet restore` after a version bump still regenerates the file
         instead of hard-failing. -->
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true' or '$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- The path to SageFs.Core.dll for a sample that wants the holder API
       (Holder / HolderRegistry / RegisteredHolder), so a sample can demonstrate
       registered live state without hand-writing a HintPath to a build output.

       Resolved HERE, in the repo's own props, rather than in each sample: a
       per-sample HintPath into SageFs.Core/bin is a build-order dependency on a
       path that may not exist yet, and the local gate proved it — in a clean
       checkout the ConsoleTicker failed with FS0039 `HolderRegistry is not
       defined`, because SageFs.Core had not been built into that Configuration.

       A path that does not exist yet simply leaves the property empty, and the
       sample's Reference is conditioned on it being non-empty, so this is safe
       on a machine where Core has never been built.

       This is a stepping stone, not the answer. The answer is SageFs injecting
       the reference at build time for a RUN APP, via
       `-p:CustomAfterMicrosoftCommonTargets=<generated>.targets` — verified to
       build a project with no Reference at all. `-p:ReferencePath=` does NOT
       work for that: it only tells MSBuild where to look for references that
       already exist, so it can never add one.

       The default is set FIRST, because `Configuration` is not yet defined when
       a .props file is evaluated — it arrives later from the SDK. Without this
       default the `Exists(...)` below tests the literal path
       `bin//net10.0/...`, which never exists, the property silently stays
       empty, and the sample fails with FS0039 rather than a clear diagnostic.

       THE PROPERTY IS NOT CONDITIONED ON THE FILE EXISTING. It used to be, and
       that made the whole mechanism a no-op on a clean checkout — which is the
       ONLY place that matters:

         <PropertyGroup Condition="Exists('…/SageFs.Core.dll')">
           <SageFsCoreAssembly>…</SageFsCoreAssembly>
         </PropertyGroup>

       With that condition, a clean checkout has no `SageFs.Core.dll`, so the
       property is empty, so the sample's `<Reference Condition="…!=''">` is
       skipped, and so the `BuildSageFsCoreFirst` target that would BUILD Core
       is skipped by the very same condition. Nothing builds Core and nothing
       references it, and the sample fails with

         error FS0039: The value, namespace, type or module
         'RegisteredHolder' is not defined

       Reproduced in ~6s from a clean checkout, and it took the release gate
       down. It was invisible locally for the obvious reason: a local checkout
       always has a stale `SageFs.Core.dll` on disk, so the condition was true
       and the stale assembly satisfied the reference.

       So the property states the PATH unconditionally and lets the build decide
       whether the file is there. The ordering target then builds Core when it
       is missing, and a missing HintPath after that is a real error rather than
       a silently-skipped reference. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Configuration Condition="'$(Configuration)' == ''">Debug</Configuration>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <SageFsCoreAssembly>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)SageFs.Core/bin/$(Configuration)/net10.0/SageFs.Core.dll</SageFsCoreAssembly>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>


